<sling = 'http://sling.apache.org/jcr/sling/1.0'>
<iap = 'https://iap.uhndata.io/'>
<err = 'https://iap.uhndata.io/err/'>

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Error tracking: errors that a running instance could not deal with on its
// own are recorded under /LoggedErrors, so that a system administrator can be
// told about them long after the log file they were written to has rotated
// away.
//
// Nothing here is versionable and /LoggedErrors is not under a versionable
// ancestor, so no on-parent-version attributes are needed anywhere in this
// file; their absence is deliberate rather than an oversight.
//
//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Homepage node for the /LoggedErrors root path, holding the recorded errors.
//
// An iap:EntityHomepage rather than a plain container: the recorded errors are
// not entities, but the homepage's generic tools are exactly what somebody
// working through them needs — the pagination servlet listing and filtering
// its children, and rep:AccessControllable for the ACL that repoinit places
// here.
[err:LoggedErrorsHomepage] > iap:EntityHomepage
  // Attributes:

  // We can query the recorded errors
  query

  // Properties:

  - sling:resourceType (STRING) = "err/LoggedErrorsHomepage" mandatory autocreated protected
  - sling:resourceSuperType (STRING) = "iap/EntityHomepage" mandatory autocreated protected

  // The type of the children listed by the pagination servlet
  - childNodeType (STRING) = "err:LoggedError" mandatory autocreated protected

  // Allow any other properties for extensibility
  - * (UNDEFINED)
  - * (UNDEFINED) multiple

  // Contains recorded errors, thrown or not
  + * (err:LoggedError) = err:LoggedFailure

  // Allow any other children for extensibility
  + * (nt:base) = nt:unstructured

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What every recorded error has in common, whether or not something was
// thrown. Never instantiated directly: a recording is either an
// err:LoggedFailure or an err:LoggedProblem.
//
// The node is named after a *fingerprint* of the fault rather than after its
// printed stack trace: the code that failed — the frames and the classes of
// the throwable chain — together with the component and the operation the
// caller named. Everything that varies with the data rather than with the
// fault, the message included, is deliberately outside the identity and kept
// below as a bounded sample instead. The number of these nodes is therefore
// bounded by the number of distinct ways this build can fail, not by how much
// data flows through it, which is what makes it safe never to delete any of
// them.
//
// An iap:Content, so that a recorded error is an ordinary piece of content:
// readable as JSON, listable, and taggable — which is how a decision about one
// is recorded, since nothing here is ever removed. iap:Content also brings
// sling:Folder > nt:folder > nt:hierarchyNode > mix:created, so jcr:created
// and jcr:createdBy are autocreated by the repository; jcr:created is when the
// fault was first seen, and `lastOccurrence` when it was last seen.
[err:LoggedError] > iap:Content
  // Attributes:

  // This is an abstract node type, a recording is one of the two below
  abstract

  // We can query the recorded errors
  query

  // Properties:

  // Hardcode the supertype to the parent err/LoggedError type, so that both
  // kinds of recording answer to it. Without this the subtypes would inherit
  // iap:Content's value and their resource type chain would skip straight past
  // err/LoggedError — which is what the acknowledge servlet is bound to, what
  // the triage tags are targeted at, and how the container picks its children
  // out.
  - sling:resourceSuperType (STRING) = "err/LoggedError" mandatory autocreated protected

  // The class doing the work when the fault happened — for a plugin, the
  // plugin's own class rather than the framework that called it, since that is
  // what has to be fixed. Part of the fingerprint, so it is either a class
  // name or absent, never anything derived from content. Guessed from the
  // topmost frame that is ours when the caller did not say.
  - component (STRING)

  // What the component was trying to do: a short label chosen in code, such as
  // "computeTags" or "readDefinitions". Part of the fingerprint, same rule — a
  // constant, never a path or an identifier.
  - operation (STRING)

  // How many times this fault has been recorded so far. Not a count of the
  // samples below: a fault recorded a thousand times keeps twenty subjects,
  // and this says a thousand.
  - occurrences (LONG) = '1' mandatory autocreated

  // When this fault was last recorded; jcr:created is when it was first seen
  - lastOccurrence (DATE) mandatory

  // A bounded sample of the distinct messages this fault was seen with, most
  // recent first. Multi-valued because whatever varies between occurrences is
  // outside the fingerprint: "Cannot read /Submissions/1234" and "Cannot read
  // /Submissions/5678" are one fault seen twice, not two faults. For a thrown
  // failure these are the throwable's messages; for a problem, the phrases the
  // caller reported that were too variable to name the fault by.
  - messages (STRING) multiple

  // A bounded sample of the paths this fault happened to, most recent first,
  // and only a sample: there is deliberately no count of how many distinct
  // paths were hit, because keeping one would mean either keeping them all or
  // making a number up. Diagnostic evidence, not a work queue — content that
  // has to be retried or repaired must carry its own marker for that, the way
  // a node whose tags could not be computed carries tagComputationFailed.
  - subjects (STRING) multiple

  // A bounded sample of the users this fault happened on behalf of, most
  // recent first. Absent for anything running outside a request: a commit
  // hook, a scheduled task, an asynchronous listener.
  - actors (STRING) multiple

  // Everything else the caller said about the most recent occurrence, one
  // "key: value" per line. Overwritten on each occurrence rather than
  // accumulated, so it stays the size of a single occurrence: it answers "what
  // exactly was going on the last time this happened", which is what is needed
  // to reproduce it, and nothing more.
  - lastContext (STRING)

  // Allow any other properties for extensibility
  - * (UNDEFINED)
  - * (UNDEFINED) multiple

  // The decisions somebody has taken about this error
  + * (err:Acknowledgement) = err:Acknowledgement

  // Allow any other children for extensibility
  + * (nt:base) = nt:unstructured

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A recorded error that something was thrown for.
[err:LoggedFailure] > err:LoggedError
  - sling:resourceType (STRING) = "err/LoggedFailure" mandatory autocreated protected

  // The class name of the outermost throwable, e.g.
  // "java.lang.IllegalStateException". Part of the fingerprint.
  - type (STRING) mandatory

  // The full stack trace of one occurrence, causes included, as it would be
  // printed to a log file, truncated if enormous. An exemplar rather than the
  // identity: every occurrence shares these frames by construction, but may
  // carry a different message.
  - stackTrace (STRING) mandatory

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A recorded error that nothing was thrown for: something the instance found
// wrong and could only shrug at, most often a mis-authored definition — a
// condition naming a comparator that does not exist, an operand with no
// resolver behind it. There is no stack trace worth showing for these: where
// the code noticed is in `component` and `operation`, and what it was looking
// at is in `subjects`.
[err:LoggedProblem] > err:LoggedError
  - sling:resourceType (STRING) = "err/LoggedProblem" mandatory autocreated protected

  // What is wrong: a short phrase chosen in code, such as "unknown
  // comparator". Part of the fingerprint, so the same rule as `operation` — a
  // constant, with whatever varies left to `subjects`. A caller who reports a
  // phrase that quotes something is recorded under its stable head, and the
  // phrase itself is kept in `messages`: dropping the recording would make the
  // module that exists to catch silent failures commit one.
  - problem (STRING) mandatory

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One decision somebody took about one recorded error.
//
// A child node rather than a property, and appended rather than replaced,
// because a decision is exactly the kind of thing the reviews and audits this
// module exists for need to be able to look back at: who decided what, when,
// why, and how much had happened by then. It is also what lets a fault
// un-acknowledge itself — see `acknowledgedOccurrences`.
[err:Acknowledgement] > iap:Content
  - sling:resourceType (STRING) = "err/Acknowledgement" mandatory autocreated protected

  // What was decided, the name of a tag in the error-triage category, e.g.
  // "known-issue". The triage tags on the error itself are derived from the
  // most recent decision, never placed by hand, so this is where a decision is
  // actually made.
  - resolution (STRING) mandatory

  // Why, in the acknowledger's own words
  - note (STRING)

  // What the error's `occurrences` had reached when this decision was taken.
  // An error counts as dealt with only while it has not happened again since,
  // so a fault that recurs after somebody decided it was fixed goes back to
  // needing attention on its own, without anything having to notice the date.
  // A count rather than a timestamp because a count cannot be confused by two
  // things happening in the same second, or by a clock that disagrees.
  - acknowledgedOccurrences (LONG) mandatory

  // jcr:created and jcr:createdBy, from iap:Content, record when and by whom

  // Allow any other properties for extensibility
  - * (UNDEFINED)
  - * (UNDEFINED) multiple

  // Allow any other children for extensibility
  + * (nt:base) = nt:unstructured
